{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeightVanishing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeightVanishing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.Young.YoungDiagram","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.GroupWithZero.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.GroupWithZero.Unbundled.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Units.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":27,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.not_isOcneanuWeightSystem_const_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.not_isOcneanuWeightSystem_const_one","kind":"theorem","line":533,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L533-L549","doc":"**The class is not vacuous: it rules things out.**\n\nThe constant function `1` is *not* an Ocneanu weight system at `Q = 2` in `ℚ`,\nat any level `2` and any parameter — because it fails to vanish on the\nthree-row diagram `(1³)`, which `ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl` forces it\nto. So the class genuinely constrains `y`; it is not a label that any function\ncan wear. Together with `isOcneanuWeightSystem_schurPrincipal` this brackets\nthe hypothesis from both sides. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.isOcneanuWeightSystem_schurPrincipal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.isOcneanuWeightSystem_schurPrincipal","kind":"theorem","line":516,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L516-L531","doc":"**The class is satisfiable** — the hypothesis is not empty, so the\nconditional theorems above are not vacuous.\n\n**This does not discharge Wenzl's theorem, and must not be read as doing so.**\nIt says only that the *defined function* `schurPrincipal` satisfies, by\n`rfl`, the property of being its own principal specialisation. Wenzl's content\nis that the **Markov trace's** weights have this form — a statement about an\nobject this file cannot name. Deliberately **not** an `instance`, so typeclass\nresolution can never use it to silently discharge an\n`[IsOcneanuWeightSystem …]` hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_ocneanuWeight_ne_zero_of_two_rows_of_wenzl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_ocneanuWeight_ne_zero_of_two_rows_of_wenzl","kind":"theorem","line":498,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L498-L507","doc":"The surviving side for the actual weight — conditional on Wenzl 1988.\nA diagram with at most two rows has nonzero Ocneanu weight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl","kind":"theorem","line":466,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L466-L496","doc":"**The all-`n` Temperley–Lieb weight-vanishing lemma for the actual Ocneanu\nweight — conditional on Wenzl 1988.**\n\nAt convention D, `y_λ = 0 ⟺ λ has at least three rows`. This is the statement\n#5207 named, stated about the object #5207 meant (the trace's Wedderburn\nweights) rather than about the hook-content formula, and it is **conditional**:\nthe `[IsOcneanuWeightSystem]` hypothesis is Wenzl's theorem.\n\nBoth class fields are consumed, neither trivially:\n\n* `markovParameter_eq` → `level_eq_two_of_conventionD` turns convention D's\n  parameter into the integer `r = 2`;\n* `weight_eq_schurPrincipal` → transports the unconditional vanishing criterion\n  to `y`.\n\nCite as *\"proved conditional on Wenzl 1988\"*, never as *\"proved\"*.\n\n`z` and `r` are explicit: neither appears in the conclusion, so typeclass\nresolution cannot recover them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl","kind":"theorem","line":446,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L446-L464","doc":"**The weight-vanishing criterion, conditional on Wenzl 1988.**\n\n`y μ = 0 ↔ ℓ(μ) > r`, for the *actual* Ocneanu weight system `y` — as opposed\nto `schurPrincipal_eq_zero_iff`, which says the same of the formula and is\nunconditional.\n\nDerived, not projected: the class supplies `y = schurPrincipal / Z`, and all\nthe mathematical content comes from `schurPrincipal_eq_zero_iff`.\n\n`z` is an explicit argument: it appears in the hypothesis but not in the\nconclusion, so typeclass resolution cannot recover it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_eq_two_of_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_eq_two_of_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":423,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L423-L442","doc":"**Convention D pins the level to the integer `r = 2`.**\n\nGiven that a trace's Markov parameter is Wenzl's at level `r`, and that the\nparameter is convention D's (`Q = q²`, `z = q·z_D`, the factor `q` being the\nHecke-normalisation shift of `wenzlLevel_conventionD`), the level *is* `2`.\n\nUnconditional: this is `wenzlLevel_conventionD`'s algebra plus\n`level_unique_of_markovParameter`. It is what\n`conventionD_ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl` uses to consume the class's\n`markovParameter_eq` field — so that field does real work rather than\ndecorating the class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_unique_of_markovParameter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_unique_of_markovParameter","kind":"theorem","line":396,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L396-L421","doc":"**`Q^r` is determined by the Markov parameter, and hence so is `r`.**\n\nIf two levels `r` and `s` sit at the same parameter `z` then `r = s`, for `Q`\nnot a root of unity. So \"which Wenzl level is this trace at?\" has at most one\nanswer — which is what lets `level_eq_two_of_conventionD` below turn convention\nD's parameter into the *integer* 2 rather than merely into `Q^r = Q²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.pow_level_mul_eq_of_markovParameter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.pow_level_mul_eq_of_markovParameter","kind":"theorem","line":387,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L387-L394","doc":"Wenzl's parameter relation, solved for `Q^r`: `Q^r(1 − Q + z) = z`.\n\nPure algebra — no hypothesis at all, not even `Q ≠ 0`. This is the inversion\n`Q^r = z/(1 − Q + z)` quoted in the file header, in the form that avoids\ndividing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem.weight_eq_schurPrincipal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem.weight_eq_schurPrincipal","kind":"theorem","line":382,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L382-L382","doc":"**Wenzl 1988.** The weights are the level-`r` principal specialisations,\nup to a nonzero normaliser. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem.markovParameter_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem.markovParameter_eq","kind":"theorem","line":379,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L379-L379","doc":"The trace's Markov parameter is Wenzl's at level `r`, denominators\ncleared: `z(1 − Q^r) = Q^r(1 − Q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem.mk","kind":"ctor","line":376,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L376-L376","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.IsOcneanuWeightSystem","kind":"class","line":348,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L348-L383","doc":"**Wenzl (1988), as an explicit hypothesis: `y` is the Ocneanu weight system\nat level `r`.**\n\nThe class carries Wenzl's *hypothesis*, not the vanishing conclusion. Its two\nfields say:\n\n1. `markovParameter_eq` — the trace's Markov parameter `z` is Wenzl's at level\n   `r`, i.e. `z = Q^r(1−Q)/(1−Q^r)`, cleared of denominators.\n2. `weight_eq_schurPrincipal` — **the external input.** At that parameter the\n   weights are the level-`r` principal specialisations, up to the normaliser\n   `Z` fixed by `Σ_λ f^λ y_λ = tr(1) = 1`. Only `Z ≠ 0` is used, so the\n   statement does not depend on which normalisation convention is in force.\n\nNothing about *vanishing* appears here. The conclusion `y μ = 0 ↔ ℓ(λ) > r` is\n**derived** — from this class plus the unconditional\n`schurPrincipal_eq_zero_iff` — rather than projected out of a field. That is\nthe §3b-cond non-degeneracy requirement, and it is the whole point: the\nmathematical content of the vanishing lemma stays where it was proved, and the\nclass supplies only the bridge.\n\n`y` is opaque. Lean cannot yet see the Hecke algebra or its trace, so the class\ncannot *quantify over* the actual Ocneanu weight — it can only name the\nproperty a candidate `y` would have to have. Producing an instance for the real\ntrace weights is exactly Wenzl's theorem, and is exactly what remains\nunformalised.\n-- Ref: [wenzl1988] https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01404457\n   Wenzl, \"Hecke algebras of type $A_n$ and subfactors\", Invent. Math. 92\n   (1988) 349–383. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.discriminates_at_n_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.discriminates_at_n_three","kind":"theorem","line":298,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L298-L314","doc":"**The criterion discriminates.** At `n = 3` over `ℚ` with `Q = 2`, the\nthree-row diagram `(1³)` has weight zero and the two-row diagram `(2,1)` does\nnot. These are the two blocks PR #5185 measured — `y₍₁³₎ = 0`,\n`y₍₂,₁₎ = q²/(q²+1)² ≠ 0` — so the Lean returns the measured verdict on the\nmeasured case, and it is not returning \"zero\" for everything. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_two_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_two_one","kind":"theorem","line":291,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L291-L296","doc":"`(2,1)` — two rows — has `colLen 0 = 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_one_one_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_one_one_one","kind":"theorem","line":284,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L284-L289","doc":"`(1,1,1)` — three rows — has `colLen 0 = 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.not_root_of_unity_of_pos_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.not_root_of_unity_of_pos_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":274,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L274-L282","doc":"**The genericity hypothesis holds at every positive `Q ≠ 1`**, hence in\nparticular at the substrate `Q = q₀²` (`q₀ ≈ 1.10998`, so `Q ≈ 1.232`). This\nis the archimedean instantiation: it lives after the substrate→archimedean\nwall and is not used by any statement above it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_ne_zero_of_two_rows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_ne_zero_of_two_rows","kind":"theorem","line":256,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L256-L263","doc":"The surviving side, stated positively: a diagram with at most two rows has\nnonzero weight. This is the half that makes the criterion sharp — without it\n\"the `≥ 3`-row blocks die\" would be compatible with everything dying. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff_rowLens","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff_rowLens","kind":"theorem","line":248,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L248-L254","doc":"The same statement with \"at least three rows\" spelled as the length of the\nrow-length list, which is how `λ`'s number of rows is usually written. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":223,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L223-L246","doc":"**The all-`n` Temperley–Lieb weight-vanishing lemma, at the level of the\nformula.**\n\nAt convention D — equivalently, by `wenzlLevel_conventionD`, at Wenzl level\n`r = 2` — the normalised level-2 principal specialisation of `λ` vanishes\n**iff** `λ` has at least three rows. `Z` is the normaliser `Σ_μ f^μ s_μ`\nenforcing `Σ_λ f^λ y_λ = tr(1) = 1`; only its non-vanishing is used, so the\nstatement is independent of which normalisation convention is in force.\n\n**Unconditional, and about `schurPrincipal` — not about the Ocneanu weight.**\nThis says nothing about the Markov trace on `H_n(Q)`; it is a fact about the\nhook-content product. The bridge to the actual weight is Wenzl 1988, carried by\n`IsOcneanuWeightSystem`; the corresponding statement about the weight itself is\n`conventionD_ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl`, which is conditional.\n\nThis is the statement PR #5207 named. It holds for every `n` and every `λ`,\nand it is the reason `radical = ⟨E⁽³⁾⟩`: the blocks the trace annihilates are\nexactly the `≥ 3`-row ones, whose dimensions sum to `n! − Catalan(n)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L173-L219","doc":"**The vanishing criterion, for every `n`, every `λ` and every level `r`.**\n\nThe level-`r` principal specialisation vanishes exactly when `λ` has more than\n`r` rows:\n\n`s_λ(1, Q, …, Q^{r−1}) = 0 ⟺ ℓ(λ) > r`.\n\n`Q` is assumed not to be a root of unity — precisely the genericity under\nwhich `H_n(Q)` is semisimple and the weights are defined. Without it the\nstatement is false, so the hypothesis is doing real work rather than\ndecorating the signature: at a root of unity a *hook length* can also kill a\ndenominator and further numerator factors can vanish off content `−r`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_gt_of_content_eq_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_gt_of_content_eq_neg","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L162-L171","doc":"A cell of `μ` of content `−r` forces `μ` to contain `(r, 0)`, hence to have\nmore than `r` rows. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.wenzlLevel_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.wenzlLevel_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L141-L158","doc":"**Convention D is Wenzl level `r = 2`.**\n\nWenzl's parameter relation `z_W = Q^r(1−Q)/(1−Q^r)` inverts to\n`Q^r = z_W/(1 − Q + z_W)`. Convention D sits at `Q = q²` and `z_W = q·z_D`\n(the factor `q` is the Hecke-normalisation shift between\n`g² = (Q−1)g + Q` and `σ² = (q − q⁻¹)σ + 1`, under which `σ = g/q` and\n`Q = q²`). Substituting `z_D = q³/(q²+1)` gives `Q^r = q⁴ = Q²`, so `r = 2`\non the nose — the SU(2) level, whose row cap is the Temperley–Lieb one.\n\nNot cited: this is an algebraic identity in `q`, so it is proved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal","kind":"def","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L116-L139","doc":"The **level-`r` principal specialisation** of the Schur function, in the\nhook-content product form used throughout the corpus:\n\n`s_λ(1, Q, …, Q^{r−1}) = Q^{n(λ)} · ∏_{x ∈ λ} (1 − Q^{r + c(x)}) / (1 − Q^{h(x)})`\n\nThis is `markov_weights_symbolic.schur_principal_symbolic` and\n`schur_principal_mpmath._schur_principal_with`, transcribed verbatim.\n\nWenzl's theorem — the external input, and the only one — is that these *are*\nthe Ocneanu/Markov trace weights on `H_n(Q)` at Markov parameter\n`z_W = Q^r(1−Q)/(1−Q^r)`, up to the normalisation `Σ_λ f^λ y_λ = 1`.\n-- Ref: [wenzl1988] https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01404457\n   Wenzl, \"Hecke algebras of type $A_n$ and subfactors\", Invent. Math. 92\n   (1988) 349–383 — the source of the weight formula.\n-- Ref: [orellana1998] https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9806163\n   Orellana, \"Weights of Markov Traces on Hecke Algebras\", p. 17, restates\n   Wenzl's type-A result in exactly this form: *\"In [W1] Wenzl showed that\n   the weights of the Markov trace (with parameter `z = q^r(1−q)/(1−q^r)`)\n   on the Hecke algebra of type A are given by the symmetric Schur functions\n   `s_{λ,r}(q)`.\"* **Caution:** Orellana's \"type D\" is the *Coxeter* type\n   `D_n` and has nothing to do with this repo's trace convention D. Only the\n   type-A statement above is used. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.one_le_hook","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.one_le_hook","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L102-L110","doc":"Every cell of a diagram has hook length at least `1`: its arm and its leg\nare each at least `0`, and the `+1` counts the cell itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.nPart","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.nPart","kind":"def","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L98-L100","doc":"`n(λ) = Σ_i (i−1)·λ_i`, i.e. the sum of the 0-indexed row indices over the\ncells. Matches `markov_weights_symbolic.n_partition`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.hook","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.hook","kind":"def","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L93-L96","doc":"The **hook length** `h(i, j) = arm + leg + 1` of a cell of `μ`. Matches\n`computations/markov/markov_weights_symbolic.hooks_and_contents`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.content","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.content","kind":"def","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L87-L91","doc":"The **content** of a cell, `c(i, j) = j − i`, as an integer (cells are\n0-indexed, matching `YoungDiagram`). Contents in the first column are\n`0, −1, −2, …`, so a cell of content `−r` exists exactly when the diagram has\nmore than `r` rows — the fact the whole file turns on. ","signatureChunk":0}]}